-- title:
--   Factoring a point of the laxer deformation ring through θ
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a complete discrete valuation domain (complete for its maximal-adic topology), let $\bar\rho$ be a two-dimensional representation of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ over the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$ that factors through a finite level, and let $\mathcal{D}_0$ and $\mathcal{D}'$ be two deformation conditions, i.e. predicates on rank-two adically continuous Galois representations over local $\mathcal{O}$-algebras. Let $D_0$ and $D'$ be `DeformationRingData` for $(\mathcal{O},\bar\rho)$ with respect to $\mathcal{D}_0$ and $\mathcal{D}'$: each consists of a complete Noetherian local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with local structure map whose residue field is generated by the image of $\mathcal{O}$, together with a representation over it of the given type whose residual representation is equivalent to the base change of $\bar\rho$, universal among all such deformations over complete Noetherian local $\mathcal{O}$-algebras. Suppose $\theta : D'.R \to D_0.R$ is a local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism such that the base change of $D'.\rho$ along $\theta$ is isomorphic to $D_0.\rho$ as a Galois representation. Let $A$ be a complete Noetherian local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with local structure map such that the composite $\mathcal{O} \to A \to A/\mathfrak{m}_A$ is surjective, and let $x_\varphi : D'.R \to A$ be a local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism whose associated deformation, the base change of $D'.\rho$ along $x_\varphi$, satisfies both $\mathcal{D}'$ and $\mathcal{D}_0$. Then there exists a local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : D_0.R \to A$ with $\psi \circ \theta = x_\varphi$. Neither uniqueness of $\psi$ nor the equivalence of the base change of $D_0.\rho$ along $\psi$ with the given representation is asserted.
--
--   This is the comparison of two universal deformation rings for nested deformation conditions: a point of the ring governing the laxer condition whose associated deformation also satisfies the stricter one factors through the map $\theta$ from the laxer to the stricter universal ring. It is used in the local analysis of the cotangent spaces and lengths of level quotients that underlies the numerical criterion.
